<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Mosque of al-Nuri (Mosul)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1172–2017</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-al-nuri">Great Mosque of al-Nuri</a> was a mosque in the old city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, built by the Zengid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a> in 1172. Its leaning brick minaret, al-Hadba, &quot;the hunchback&quot;, became the symbol of the city. In 2014 the Islamic State leader Abu Bakr al-Baghdadi proclaimed his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> there, and the group blew up the mosque in 2017; its reconstruction was agreed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 2018.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-al-nuri-mosul.jpg" alt="Great Mosque of al-Nuri (Mosul)" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: الدبوني · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Mosque_of_al-Nuri_Feb_2025_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
